From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fingerprint Lab <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 14:40:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] goodix550c: tell a lifted finger apart from a mis-placed one

An enrolment session over fprintd produced nineteen rejected stages. Eighteen
of them measured 95.1% to 95.3% non-contact and one measured 25.6%. Every one
of them told the operator that the finger was not centred, so the session was
read as the smaller finger covering too little of the pad, and the real
distinction was invisible.

A frame at 95% is not partial contact. It holds no contact at all: the finger
had already left the pad when the image was read, which the same figure has
shown across sessions and across fingers. Only the 25.6% frame was a placement
fault.

Report the two separately. Above GOODIX_ENROLL_NO_CONTACT_FRACTION the retry
becomes FP_DEVICE_RETRY_TOO_SHORT, which asks for a longer hold; below it the
existing centre-the-finger retry is unchanged. The coverage gate itself is not
moved, because the templates it rejects are genuinely unusable either way.

---

--- a/drivers/goodix53x5/goodix53x5-enroll.c
+++ b/drivers/goodix53x5/goodix53x5-enroll.c
@@ -99,13 +99,23 @@
          * to re-place the finger instead of storing such a stage. */
         if (self->captured_clipped_fraction > GOODIX_ENROLL_MAX_CLIPPED_FRACTION)
           {
+            /* A frame with essentially no contact is not a placement fault:
+             * the finger had already been lifted when the image was read.
+             * Asking for it to be centred sends the operator hunting a problem
+             * they do not have, so ask for a longer hold instead. */
+            gboolean no_contact = self->captured_clipped_fraction >
+                                  GOODIX_ENROLL_NO_CONTACT_FRACTION;
+
             fp_dbg ("Enrollment stage rejected: %.1f%% of frame has no "
-                    "finger contact (limit %.1f%%)",
+                    "finger contact (limit %.1f%%, %s)",
                     self->captured_clipped_fraction * 100.0,
-                    GOODIX_ENROLL_MAX_CLIPPED_FRACTION * 100.0);
+                    GOODIX_ENROLL_MAX_CLIPPED_FRACTION * 100.0,
+                    no_contact ? "finger already lifted" : "partial placement");
             g_clear_pointer (&self->captured_image, g_free);
             fpi_device_enroll_progress (dev, self->enroll_stage, NULL,
-                                        fpi_device_retry_new (FP_DEVICE_RETRY_CENTER_FINGER));
+                                        fpi_device_retry_new (no_contact ?
+                                                              FP_DEVICE_RETRY_TOO_SHORT :
+                                                              FP_DEVICE_RETRY_CENTER_FINGER));
             fpi_ssm_next_state (ssm);
             return;
           }
--- a/drivers/goodix53x5/goodix53x5-private.h
+++ b/drivers/goodix53x5/goodix53x5-private.h
@@ -94,6 +94,14 @@
  * a retry so stored templates keep full ridge coverage. */
 #define GOODIX_ENROLL_MAX_CLIPPED_FRACTION 0.10
 
+/* Above this, the frame holds no contact at all rather than partial contact:
+ * the finger had already left the pad when the image was read. Measured
+ * repeatedly at 95.1-95.3% across sessions and fingers, against genuine partial
+ * placements that land far lower. The two cases need different advice, because
+ * telling somebody to centre a finger that is no longer touching sends them
+ * chasing a placement problem they do not have. */
+#define GOODIX_ENROLL_NO_CONTACT_FRACTION 0.90
+
 /* PSK white box for writing all-zero PSK */
 #define GOODIX_PSK_WHITE_BOX_LEN 96
 

